      <h2 id="experience-heading">Pick a cabin for how you will use it.</h2>

      <h3>Traveling with teenagers</h3>
      <p>Cabins with a door between them feel safe. But two balconies cost a lot. Maybe the adults want a balcony and the teens won't use one. Then an inside cabin right across the hall is worth a look. I check ages, cabin spots, and Royal Caribbean's booking rules before I suggest it.</p>

      <h3>What worked for us on Allure</h3>
      <p>On Allure of the Seas, we picked two linked Central Park View cabins. Their windows look out over the park. They were plenty for us. We spent our time out on the ship, not in our rooms.</p>

      <h3>Space matters, too</h3>
      <p>On Utopia of the Seas, our cabin felt roomier. That is part of what I help you weigh. Look at the layout, the price, and how much time you will spend in the room.</p>

      <p class="about__takeaway">It comes down to cost and use. On our first call, I help you decide if a balcony, more space, or a linked cabin is worth it for your group.</p>
